[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Creational Patterns: Singleton and Factory"},{"order":2,"type":"subheading","text":"Introduction to Creational Patterns"},{"order":3,"type":"paragraph","text":"Creational design patterns deal with object creation mechanisms, trying to create objects in a manner suitable to the situation. They provide various object creation mechanisms, which increase flexibility and reuse of existing code."},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"This tutorial will cover two fundamental creational patterns: Singleton and Factory."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"Singleton Pattern"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Singleton pattern ensures that a class has only one instance and provides a global point of access to it. It restricts the instantiation of a class to one object."},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"This is useful when exactly one object is needed to coordinate actions across the system."},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Implementation (Java Example)"},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"public class Singleton {
    private static Singleton instance;

    private Singleton() { // Private constructor to prevent instantiation from outside
    }

    public static Singleton getInstance() {
        if (instance == null) {
            instance = new Singleton();
        }
        return instance;
    }

    public void doSomething() {
        System.out.println("Singleton is doing something!");
    }
}

// Usage:
Singleton singleton = Singleton.getInstance();
singleton.doSomething();"},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Private Constructor: Prevents direct instantiation of the class."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Static Instance: Holds the single instance of the class."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"getInstance() Method: Provides a global access point to the instance. It creates the instance if it doesn't exist yet."},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Factory Pattern"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Factory pattern provides an interface for creating objects in a super class, but allows subclasses to alter the type of objects that will be created. It encapsulates object creation logic."},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"This pattern is useful when you need to create different types of objects based on some condition or input, without specifying the exact class to instantiate."},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"Implementation (Java Example)"},{"order":17,"type":"code","text":"// Interface for products
interface Animal {
    void makeSound();
}

// Concrete products
class Dog implements Animal {
    @Override
    public void makeSound() {
        System.out.println("Woof!");
    }
}

class Cat implements Animal {
    @Override
    public void makeSound() {
        System.out.println("Meow!");
    }
}

// Factory class
class AnimalFactory {
    public Animal createAnimal(String type) {
        if ("dog".equalsIgnoreCase(type)) {
            return new Dog();
        } else if ("cat".equalsIgnoreCase(type)) {
            return new Cat();
        } else {
            return null; // Or throw an exception
        }
    }
}

// Usage:
AnimalFactory factory = new AnimalFactory();
Animal dog = factory.createAnimal("dog");
dog.makeSound(); // Output: Woof!
Animal cat = factory.createAnimal("cat");
cat.makeSound(); // Output: Meow!"},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Product Interface: Defines the interface for the objects the factory creates."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Concrete Products: Implement the product interface."},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Factory Class: Contains the logic for creating different types of products based on input."},{"order":21,"type":"subheading","text":"Benefits of Using These Patterns"},{"order":22,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Singleton: Controlled access to a single instance, resource management."},{"order":23,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Factory: Decoupling of object creation, increased flexibility, easier maintenance."},{"order":24,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":25,"type":"paragraph","text":"Singleton and Factory are powerful creational patterns that can significantly improve the design and maintainability of your code. Understanding and applying these patterns appropriately can lead to more robust and flexible applications."}]
